cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this specification, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Shared Agent Context Memory (SACM): A Governance-First Specification for AI Agent Context Memory"
abstract: >
  SACM (Shared Agent Context Memory) is a specification for how AI-assisted work is kept
  organised in one shared memory, so that humans can audit it and stay responsible for the
  result. It recognizes that agent memory is fundamentally
  an authorization problem and applies established access control and audit-log
  patterns to this domain. The same model serves one person and an organisation: a person
  working alone is a team of one. The main proposal is the Mandate Gate: a pre-work
  mandate, confirmed by the principal, must exist in the agent's memory before work
  is a governed interval. SACM defines three memory tiers organized by trust level
  (intent / trace / evidence), capture-author separation, a memory kept in
  a repository of its own and bound to repositories of work by identity, scopes,
  collision-safe shared recording, and 21 numbered
  requirements with specified checks. SACM implements the Principles of Earned
  Autonomy (PEA) for the context memory design problem: PEA is the theory and its principles are
  SACM's one dependency. SACM stands on its own and can be implemented without the PEA skills. The specification claims
  no implementation conformance: every requirement is Proposed.
